WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.640
Gold Rush has been on the air for over a&nbsp;
decade, and cast members of come and gone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.160
Would you believe teenagers have&nbsp;
outclassed miners 3 times their age?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.720
Here are the most successful&nbsp;
Gold Rush characters, ranked.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.760
Former Army medic Fred Lewis first appeared&nbsp;
on Parker Schnabel's spinoff series Gold Rush:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.760
Parker's Trail. He was hired to replace&nbsp;
Schnabel's former operator Rick Ness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.160
on a dangerous expedition into Papua New&nbsp;
Guinea. He joined the main show in Season 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.880
as he and his rookie team sought to&nbsp;
take advantage of the record high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.280
prices of gold and record low prices of&nbsp;
oil brought on by the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.960
"We're not just looking to have a&nbsp;
good season. I wanna make the Lewis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.720
family name and the Madden&nbsp;
family name well known."

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.480
Despite being an impressively bearded&nbsp;
and patriotic group of would-be miners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.560
Lewis and his crew lack the aptitude and ability&nbsp;
to learn skills that would save their season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.560
Even a visit from series fixer Freddy Dodge&nbsp;
wasn't able to get Team Lewis back on track.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.360
In the end, the Lewis crew's total yield was&nbsp;
just 6.4 ounces. With a worth of about $11,400,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
the team earned less than half of what the&nbsp;
Hoffman crew mined in their first season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.360
Despite his failures, Lewis has returned for&nbsp;
Season 12 as well as the 2021 limited series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.920
Gold Rush: Winter's Fortune. Will his luck&nbsp;
improve with experience? Only time will tell.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.040
Veteran miner and quarry expert Dave&nbsp;
Turin was brought in during Season 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.240
to assist his friend Todd Hoffman's&nbsp;
fledgling attempts at finding gold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.560
For three years he assisted the Hoffman crew&nbsp;
and was integral to their continued success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.200
After Hoffman's ill-fated expedition into Guyana&nbsp;
in Season 4, Turin ended their partnership and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.680
teamed up with Freddy Dodge on a new mining&nbsp;
operation in the Klondike. In Season 5, Hoffman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.720
convinced Turin to rejoin his crew, in exchange&nbsp;
for a 50% ownership stake in Hoffman's new claim.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.520
In 2019, he starred in the Gold Rush spinoff,&nbsp;
Dave Turin's Lost Mines, traveling across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.640
country and rehabilitating abandoned gold mines.&nbsp;
Since he was never a crew leader on Gold Rush, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.720
gold yields were not tallied. However, the hard&nbsp;
work he completed with the Hoffman crew, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.040
fact that he extended the Gold Rush franchise with&nbsp;
his own spinoff, earns him a place on this list.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.400
When miners are in trouble, they call Freddy&nbsp;
Dodge. Much like his colleague Dave Turin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.400
Dodge was on hand to assist the Hoffman crew in&nbsp;
its first two seasons before joining them full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.440
time in Season 3. After leaving Hoffman behind in&nbsp;
Guyana to work a Klondike claim with his brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.920
Dodge returned to the Hoffman crew in Season&nbsp;
5 and has appeared off and on ever since.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.400
He's often called to assist in particularly&nbsp;
dire moments, such as Rick Ness' collapsing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.640
wash plant in Season 9, or Fred&nbsp;
Lewis' failing crew in Season 11.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.800
In 2021, Dodge hosted the six-episode&nbsp;
special Freddy Dodge's Mine Rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.400
visiting underperforming gold mines across&nbsp;
the country and getting them back on track.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.240
"There's a lot of gold in this country.&nbsp;
THe old timers hit it pretty hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.480
Ya know, they found lots of large nuggets."

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.880
Like Dave Turin, Dodge's gold yields on&nbsp;
the show have not been tallied. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.120
his trusted place within every crew he works&nbsp;
with, along with his success as an ambassador&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.840
of the Gold Rush brand, earn him a place&nbsp;
among the show's most successful cast members.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.000
Miners Fred Hurt and his son Dustin, better known&nbsp;
as the Dakota Boys, were introduced in Season 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.160
They became regular cast members in Season 2,&nbsp;
when they leased the Porcupine Creek mine out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.240
from under Todd Hoffman after he missed a lease&nbsp;
payment. Free to mine the claim as they pleased,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.080
the Dakota Boys attempted to reach the&nbsp;
Glory Hole, a gold-rich deposit at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.840
bottom of a water-filled ravine which&nbsp;
Jack Hoffman had previously gone after.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.760
Their work on the Glory Hole was beset with&nbsp;
problems, from a decaying cabin on their dig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.600
site to the threat of major flooding due&nbsp;
to melting snowcaps on a nearby mountain.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.320
The Dakota Boys spent three seasons on Gold Rush&nbsp;
before setting out on multiple spin-off series:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.920
All That Glitters, then Gold Rush: White&nbsp;
Water. In their time on the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:34.880
series they yielded 523 ounces of gold in&nbsp;
total, worth over $930,000. In Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.160
they achieved a personal best yield of 280&nbsp;
ounces. In recent years Fred has taken some time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.240
away from White Water, leaving Dustin to carry&nbsp;
on the family business and the "Dakota" name.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.040
Rick Ness spent a long time as Parker&nbsp;
Schnabel's operator and foreman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.040
before striking out on his own in Season&nbsp;
9. After Todd Hoffman left the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.880
Ness took his position as the third leg&nbsp;
of Gold Rush's three-team structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.680
Known for his tattoos and sleeveless shirts, Ness'&nbsp;
time on the show has been marked with struggle. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.840
learned on the job that being a skilled foreman is&nbsp;
not the same thing as being a skilled crew leader.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.880
"This year is different. I'm the boss. It's&nbsp;
100% my operation. I've never had this much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.960
responsibility before. I've never put, ya&nbsp;
know, my own money on the line like this."

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.440
Ness has had to deal with low yields and&nbsp;
debt, as well as his team's low morale as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.680
he works to improve his performance. Ness has&nbsp;
shown he can be a responsible decision maker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.680
and he even brought Freddy Dodge in to help right&nbsp;
his operation and fix some equipment. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.040
he does make some more reckless calls as well,&nbsp;
such as re-opening a plot of land as Winter set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.360
in at the end of Season 10. In his three seasons&nbsp;
as crew leader, Ness has yielded a total of 2,732&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:46.480
ounces, worth about $4.8 million. His first season&nbsp;
yield of 1,100 ounces remains his personal best.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.240
If you revisit the early seasons of Gold Rush,&nbsp;
you'll notice a significant amount of screentime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.720
is dedicated to Todd Hoffman's inexperience&nbsp;
and failure. After his Oregon-based aviation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.880
business went under, Hoffman gathered a team&nbsp;
of other unemployed men, his father Jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.440
and a camera crew before setting out for Alaska.&nbsp;
He had a considerable amount of faith in himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.640
and a showman's instinct for good TV. However,&nbsp;
he lacked even basic gold mining experience.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.360
His first mining season ended with a yield of&nbsp;
just under 15 ounces, but Hoffman was undeterred.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.320
"We haven't accomplished our goal. We've proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.120
that you can still do stuff&nbsp;
like this here in America."

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.680
Over the course of the next two seasons, he and&nbsp;
his team improved greatly. Then came a disastrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.840
trip to Guyana in Season 4, where Hoffman and&nbsp;
his crew were utterly defeated by the South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.800
American jungle, mining just two ounces of gold&nbsp;
before an unsuccessful pivot to diamond mining.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.440
After returning to the Yukon in Season 5,&nbsp;
Hoffman's skill and attitude recovered. In Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:45.120
6, he mined a personal-best 3,032 ounces. After&nbsp;
failed attempts to open mines in Colorado and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.240
his home state of Oregon, Hoffman left Gold Rush&nbsp;
and the mining industry altogether after Season 8.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.920
He would finish the series with a total yield&nbsp;
of 8,039 ounces of gold, worth $14.3 million.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.240
Colorful Dutch miner Tony Beets first appeared&nbsp;
on the show in Season 2, when the Hoffman crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.760
relocated to Dawson City, Yukon. Before long,&nbsp;
he became a fan favorite. With his wild beard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.240
and foul-mouthed demeanor, he was seemingly&nbsp;
made to become a breakout reality TV star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.160
It helped that he was by far the most&nbsp;
experienced miner of the show's main cast.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:23.680
"For Tony, this is a bad week. 220 ounces&nbsp;
of gold, worth about $350,000 dollars."

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.200
His brusque way of doing business&nbsp;
has also earned him long standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.080
beefs with co-stars Rick Ness and Parker Schnabel.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.480
Starting out in Season 5 with an ambitious quest&nbsp;
to get a 75-year old dredge mine up and running,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.000
Beets has been running his crews with the&nbsp;
assistance of his wife Minnie and his daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.400
Monica. Like the Hoffman crew, his seasons have&nbsp;
been up and down. However, thanks to his prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.560
experience in the industry, when Beets has&nbsp;
a good season, he brings in a lot of gold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.400
To date, Beets has mined a total of 16,261&nbsp;
ounces of gold, worth $28.9 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.760
In Season 7, he managed the largest single gold&nbsp;
clean-up in the show's history to that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.560
but his personal best came in Season&nbsp;
9 with 4,400 ounces. Beets would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.960
the most consistently successful miner on the&nbsp;
show, if not for the last name on this list.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.880
When it comes to gold yields, Parker Schnabel&nbsp;
is head and shoulders above the rest. Schanbel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.600
joined the Gold Rush cast as a teenager, working&nbsp;
on one of his grandfather's old claims. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:24.000
his career on the show, Schnabel has mined an&nbsp;
incredible 39,910 ounces, worth $67.8 million.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.360
Not only has Schnabel found success as a miner,&nbsp;
he is also the longest-running, most consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.840
star of Gold Rush. Starting as a 16-year-old&nbsp;
overcharging the Hoffman crew for firewood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.920
Schnabel emerged as not just a camera-friendly&nbsp;
presence, but a mining prodigy who saved the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.320
Hoffman crew from their own incompetence on more&nbsp;
than one occasion. Inspired by his grandfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:47.760
John, a lifelong Alaskan miner, Schnabel excavated&nbsp;
the Big Nugget Creek mine on his own in Season 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.880
While that claim yielded just 34 ounces, he very&nbsp;
quickly moved on to bigger and better things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.560
Since Season 4, he has been the top earner of each&nbsp;
season, yielding a record 7,509 ounces in Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.200
11. Through the years he has faced setbacks and&nbsp;
hardships, including the death of his grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.720
but has managed to persevere and thrive&nbsp;
both in the mountains and on camera.

